ThermaCache AI: Reconciling Cache Locality and Thermal
Constraints in LLM Serving
TECHNICAL RESEARCH & EMPIRICAL BENCHMARK REPORT | NOVEMBER 2026
Author / Architect: Reyhan Singh Target Runtimes: vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM
System Layer: Layer-7 Inference Gateway Key Validation: 68% TTFT Drop, Peak Temp <63°C
1. Executive Summary & The Macro Bottleneck
Modern high-density AI data centers running large language models (LLMs) face a fundamental physical paradox: the direct
conflict between data caching efficiency (KV-cache locality) and hardware thermal/power boundaries. Standard web load
balancers (such as round-robin or least-connections) treat GPUs as black boxes, scattering user requests and destroying
KV-cache locality. Conversely, naive cache-aware routers concentrate traffic onto specific GPUs holding context in VRAM,
causing silicon junction temperatures to rapidly exceed 80°C and triggering NVIDIA OS-level thermal clock throttling.
The Concentrator vs. Spreader Paradox:
• Cache-Aware Routers (The Concentrators): Direct traffic to GPUs holding prompt memory in VRAM. Maximizes
Time-to-First-Token (TTFT) speed, but creates extreme thermal hotspots (>80°C), triggering hardware clock throttling.
• Power-Aware Routers (The Spreaders): Distribute workloads evenly across cooler GPUs. Prevents overheating, but causes 100%
KV-cache misses, forcing long context documents to re-process from scratch.
ThermaCache AI resolves this paradox by acting as a non-invasive Layer-7 inference gateway operating directly above LLM
serving runtimes. By decoupling microsecond request dispatching from out-of-band hardware telemetry, ThermaCache evaluates
a multi-objective cost score prior to routing, ensuring cache-affine nodes are selected only when the prefill speed benefit
outweighs the queueing and thermal penalty.
2. Architectural Breakdown: Decoupled Dual-Path Gateway
To prevent proxy tokenization latency and control loop oscillation, ThermaCache splits system mechanics into two decoupled
paths:
A. Microsecond Fast Path (Request Dispatch)
The gateway performs sub-token chunk fingerprinting (xxHash) and queries epoch-versioned Cuckoo filter hints pushed from
worker nodes. This allows instant detection of matching KV-cache prefixes without running CPU-heavy BPE tokenization on the
critical path, keeping proxy overhead below 150 microseconds.
B. Out-of-Band Slow Path (Telemetry & Admission Control)
Lightweight node-side daemons stream real-time NVIDIA DCGM/NVML junction temperatures, rack wattage, and active queue
depths via UDP/gRPC every 100ms. The gateway updates worker node admission weights out-of-band, avoiding synchronous
driver polling delays on the request path.
C. Power-of-Two-Choices (P2C) Joint Cost Scheduler
When a request arrives, ThermaCache randomly samples two candidate nodes (n■, n■) and evaluates a joint cost scoring
function:
Score
n, m
 = wcache · Cache_Hit
n, m
− wqueue
 · Queue_Depth
n, m
− wpower
 · Power_Penalty
n
− wmem
 · VRAM_Pressure
n, m
A PID control loop with hysteresis deadbands is applied to the scoring output, preventing traffic 'flapping' between nodes caused
by the physical thermal inertia of GPU cooling systems.
