3. Empirical CUDA Benchmark Results
ThermaCache's performance has been empirically validated across multiple live CUDA GPU test setups, ranging from
single-model workloads to multi-model co-location and cluster stress runs.
3.1 Single-Model Sequential Benchmark (Qwen2.5-0.5B on CUDA)
In a 20-request test run evaluating prompt prefix re-use on GPU hardware, ThermaCache demonstrated significant prefill latency
acceleration:
Request Type Count / Ratio TTFT Latency (s) Peak Temp (°C) Operational Outcome
Cold Start (Cache Miss) 2 / 20 (10%) 1.677s 49.0°C Initial KV-cache allocation
Warm Cache Hit 18 / 20 (90%) 0.488s - 0.555s 52.0°C 68.4% Prefill latency reduction
Total Summary 90.0% Hit Rate 0.600s Average 52.0°C Max Zero thermal clock drops
3.2 Multi-Model Co-Location Benchmark (Qwen-0.5B & Qwen-1.5B-Instruct)
To test multi-tenant isolation and eliminate 'stranded VRAM' (where unused memory sits idle on single-model nodes),
ThermaCache co-located Qwen-0.5B (Node-Alpha) and Qwen-1.5B-Instruct (Node-Beta) using CUDA Multi-Process Service
(MPS) channels and dynamic VRAM slicing.
Node ID Co-Located Model Requests Cache Hit % Avg TTFT Peak GPU Temp
Node-Alpha Qwen2.5-0.5B 10 90.0% 0.491s 63.0°C
Node-Beta Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct 10 90.0% 0.549s 63.0°C
Cluster Total Multi-Tenant Dynamic 20 90.0% 0.520s 63.0°C (Unthrottled)
3.3 Cluster Simulation & Stress Testing (Zipfian Skew & Bursts)
Under high-concurrency simulation (2,000 iterations under Zipfian request distribution), standard Concentrator routers suffered
severe thermal degradation: junction temperatures reached 95°C, triggering clock drops and pushing P99 tail latency to 17,864
ms (17.86s). ThermaCache's P2C scheduler dynamically bypassed heated nodes, capping P99 tail latency at 6,421 ms (6.42s)
— a 64% tail latency reduction — while maintaining a 91.4% cache hit rate.
